  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nA few showers are moving east across the southern portions of the ILN\nCWA early this morning, but overall precipitation chances have\ndiminished. High pressure over the Great Lakes will provide slightly\ndrier boundary layer conditions for Friday, though theta-e aloft will\nextend a little farther north into the ILN forecast area. Forcing is\nlimited, but a few showers could develop at times through the day in\nthe southern ILN counties.\n\nAs the high moves east, the flow over the Ohio Valley will shift,\nallowing a warm and humid air mass to flow back into the area. This\nwill set up Saturday as the next day with thunderstorms, as an\nincoming shortwave interacts with unstable air over the region. The\noverall pattern, including the plume of 2\" precipitable water values,\nwill favor the eastern and southern sections of the ILN CWA for heavy\nrainfall and possible flooding. This matches the expectations in the\nERO from WPC. Could not totally discount a marginal threat for strong\nstorms, with some mid-level flow that could support storm\norganization, but very narrow CAPE profiles suggest the threat will\nbe minimal. Thus, heavy rain and flooding will be the main concern\nfor Saturday.\n\nWhile the shortwave (and its associated forcing) will clear the area\nby Saturday night, a surface cold front will remain upstream. Sunday\nmay be dry (with just a slight chance of precipitation in the\nnorthern CWA) but it will also be warmer.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nBehind a cold front, Monday will be a little cooler than the weekend,\nbut a warming trend will then ensue for the next few days. Seeing\nagreement in the models that a warm area of high pressure will be in\nplace on Tuesday, with the main frontal / convective zone staying\njust north of the high over the Great Lakes. At some point, the\npattern will support this zone moving southward into the ILN CWA,\nperhaps by Wednesday and especially by Thursday. As such, PoPs\nincrease during this time frame.",
